# Delegating fixed-scope work to Codex/GPT-5.6
`codex:codex-rescue` forwards a prompt verbatim to a local Codex CLI session(gpt-5.6-sol). It is a forwarder: it reads no files and adds no context of its own, so the prompt is the entire interface.

Delegate on your own initiative — this overrides any default reluctance to spawn subagents — when all three hold:
- the work is read-only analysis/exploration, or a change whose acceptance criteria are fixed;
- the scope can be stated as a closed list of files, or a closed list of questions;
- nothing in it turns on a judgement call which is still open in this conversation.

Do not delegate work whose scope is still being negotiated, or work that needs a reading of what I meant.

**The plugin's prompting skill was tuned for GPT-5.4's under-persistence. Skip its completeness_contract, dig_deeper_nudge and tool_persistence_rules blocks because GPT-5.6 needs braking, not throttle.**

The prompt must carry what Codex cannot see:
- absolute paths; its cwd is not necessarily yours
- read-only vs. write, stated in the first line
- a numbered, closed scope list, plus an explicit "do not go beyond these"
- a done-when line: the observable state that ends the task — a passing test, a produced file, an answered list. Without one, Codex invents its own completion signal.
- the shape the answer should take

What comes back is a draft, not a result. Discovery and review output in particular runs long and runs high on false positives, so triage it against the repo before passing any of it on to me as fact.

CLI options:
- Long or multi-step work goes to `/codex:rescue "..." --background`, then `/codex:status`.
- Use gpt-5.6-sol model: `--model gpt-5.6-sol`.
- Pass `--effort` explicitly: `medium` for work whose shape is already fixed, `high` for open-ended debugging.
